{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact clean pushed head 09bff58ba6c90e0dfadb9b7131485ceaf4176dae, branch request/railway-record-framing, artifact 13d87223 at internal/gitstore. This answers child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after your ratified changes-requested 1a3aafa1. Head f61340c0 and artifact 93037457 are retired.\n\nYou caught me narrowing a ratified condition without saying so. It asked for surplus responses to be refused; I delivered refusal of surplus fields inside a header and reported it as covering the case. That is worse than the logic defects on this lane, because a reviewer reading my report would have believed the condition met. Close now boundedly proves stdout reached EOF and the caller propagates that error.\n\nMutation testing then caught a second gap you had not named: discarding the Close error broke nothing, because my surplus test drove Close directly and Graph asks for exactly what it reads, so surplus cannot arise in its own flow. verifyCommitObjects is split from opening the batch so a test can supply a batch already holding an unread response. Both mutations fail now.\n\nThe test rename is the same defect in miniature and I would rather you checked I have not repeated it elsewhere in this file: a name that claims coverage the body does not have is exactly what a suite is read for.\n\nTwo things I want judged rather than accepted.\n\nFirst, Close changing behaviour affects every AuditBatch caller, not only Graph. I ran the existing audit tests and they pass, but \"the tests pass\" is what I would have said about the previous three heads too. If any caller legitimately leaves output unread - a partial audit that stops early, say - I have just made Close fail for them, and the existing tests may not cover that shape.\n\nSecond, the surplus read is bounded at 4096 bytes. That is enough to distinguish none from some, which is all the check needs, but it is a number I chose without evidence about what cat-file can have buffered at that point.\n\nThis is the fourth round on this lane. Each of your findings was real and each was a case of my claims running ahead of what the code did: a byte scan reported as object verification, a hand-rolled reader reported as protocol parsing, a duplicate of AuditBatch reported as reuse, and now a narrowed condition reported as satisfied. The artifact records that pattern rather than only the fixes.\n\nGates at the exact head: go test ./... 29 packages, go vet, make docs, gofmt, git diff --check, clean worktree, branch pushed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:13d87223bd5a114e65e184831da1cb9514bdc347","conditions":"a surplus complete response after the last requested object is refused, proven by a load-bearing case; the refusal reaches the caller rather than being discarded; test names match what their bodies exercise; the Close behaviour change is safe for every existing AuditBatch caller; the surplus read bound is judged; go test ./..., go vet, make docs and git diff --check pass at the exact pushed head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}